(Binary and Hex Operations in English)
(This example demonstrates working with binary data, hex values, and buffer manipulation)

Print "=== Hexadecimal Literals ===".

(Numbers can be written in hex using 0x prefix)
a number called red is 0xFF.
a number called green is 0x80.
a number called blue is 0x40.
Print "Red component: {red} ({red:02X})".
Print "Green component: {green} ({green:02X})".
Print "Blue component: {blue} ({blue:02X})".

(Combine into RGB value)
a number called rgb is red multiply 65536 add green multiply 256 add blue.
Print "Combined RGB: {rgb:06X}".

Print "".
Print "=== Binary Literals ===".

(Numbers can be written in binary using 0b prefix)
a number called flags is 0b10110100.
Print "Flags: {flags} (binary: {flags:b})".
Print "Flags padded: {flags:08b}".

Print "".
Print "=== Buffer Byte Manipulation ===".

(Create a buffer for binary data)
Create a buffer called data of size 16.

(Set individual bytes by position - 1-indexed like natural English)
Set byte 1 of data to 0x48.
Set byte 2 of data to 0x65.
Set byte 3 of data to 0x6C.
Set byte 4 of data to 0x6C.
Set byte 5 of data to 0x6F.

(Read individual bytes)
a number called 'first byte' is byte 1 of data.
Print "First byte: {first byte:02X}".

(Print buffer as string - shows "Hello")
Print "Buffer contents: ".
Print data.

Print "".
Print "=== Modifying Bytes ===".

(Change a specific byte)
Set byte 5 of data to 0x21.
Print "After changing byte 5 to '!':".
Print data.

(Zero out remaining bytes explicitly)
Set byte 6 of data to 0.

Print "".
Print "=== Bitwise Operations ===".

a number called 'high bits' is 0b11110000.
a number called 'alternate bits' is 0b10101010.

(Bitwise AND)
a number called result is 'high bits' bit-and 'alternate bits'.
Print "{high bits:08b} AND {alternate bits:08b} = {result:08b}".

(Bitwise OR)
Set result to 'high bits' bit-or 'alternate bits'.
Print "{high bits:08b} OR  {alternate bits:08b} = {result:08b}".

(Bitwise XOR)
Set result to 'high bits' bit-xor 'alternate bits'.
Print "{high bits:08b} XOR {alternate bits:08b} = {result:08b}".

(Bitwise NOT - using XOR with all 1s)
Set result to 'high bits' bit-xor 0xFF.
Print "NOT {high bits:08b} = {result:08b}".

(Bit shifting)
Set result to 'high bits' bit-shift-left 2.
Print "{high bits:08b} << 2 = {result:08b}".

Set result to 'high bits' bit-shift-right 2.
Print "{high bits:08b} >> 2 = {result:08b}".

Print "".
Print "=== Practical: Simple Hex Dump ===".

(Write some test data)
Set byte 1 of data to 0xDE.
Set byte 2 of data to 0xAD.
Set byte 3 of data to 0xBE.
Set byte 4 of data to 0xEF.
Set byte 5 of data to 0xCA.
Set byte 6 of data to 0xFE.
Set byte 7 of data to 0xBA.
Set byte 8 of data to 0xBE.

Print "Hex dump of first 8 bytes:".
a number called position is 1.
While position is less than or equal to 8,
    a number called 'byte value' is byte position of data,
    Print "{byte value:02X} " without newline,
    increment position.
Print "".

Print "".
Print "=== Practical: Patching a Binary Value ===".

(Read a 32-bit value from buffer - little endian)
a number called 'lowest byte' is byte 1 of data.
a number called 'second byte' is byte 2 of data.
a number called 'third byte' is byte 3 of data.
a number called 'highest byte' is byte 4 of data.
a number called 'stored value' is 'lowest byte' add 'second byte' multiply 256 add 'third byte' multiply 65536 add 'highest byte' multiply 16777216.
Print "32-bit value at offset 0: {stored value:08X}".

(Write a new 32-bit value - little endian)
a number called 'new value' is 0x12345678.
Set byte 1 of data to 'new value' bit-and 0xFF.
Set byte 2 of data to 'new value' bit-shift-right 8 bit-and 0xFF.
Set byte 3 of data to 'new value' bit-shift-right 16 bit-and 0xFF.
Set byte 4 of data to 'new value' bit-shift-right 24 bit-and 0xFF.

Print "After patching with 0x12345678:".
Set position to 1.
While position is less than or equal to 4,
    Set 'byte value' to byte position of data,
    Print "{byte value:02X} " without newline,
    increment position.
Print "".

(Declaring one buffer from another copies its bytes, so changing the copy
 leaves data as it was)
a buffer called 'data copy' is data.
Print "Buffer contents: ".
Print 'data copy'.
set byte 1 of 'data copy' to 'A'.
Print "After setting byte 1 to 'A':".
Print 'data copy'.

Print "".
Print "=== Done ===".
